      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6y-x).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=6y-x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=6y-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{6} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-1.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
